\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

This paper formalizes recursive generation depth estimation as ordinal classification.
The perplexity tail family dominates across all nine cells ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}4$); cross-model transfer 66.4\% (d1--d3: 55.4\%), cross-domain arXiv 74.1\%, perturbation/MIA baselines 30--35\%.
Pilot accumulation experiments (25\%/50\% synthetic mixing) yield 70.1\%/69.1\% vs.\ 84.2\% full-replacement baseline ($-$14 to $-$15\,pp), but accuracy remains $2.8{\times}$ chance; preliminary 14B observations ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$, confounded) show residual d1--d3 discrimination above chance.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem Definition}
\label{sec:method:problem}

Let $f_\theta$ denote a language model and $\mathcal{D}_0$ a corpus of human-written seed text.
We define a \emph{recursive generation chain} of depth $K$ as the sequence $\mathcal{D}_0, \mathcal{D}_1, \dots, \mathcal{D}_K$, where each $\mathcal{D}_k$ ($k \geq 1$) is produced by finetuning $f_\theta$ on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ and sampling from the resulting model.
Depth $d{=}0$ denotes human-written text; depth $d{=}k$ ($k \geq 1$) denotes text generated by a model trained on depth-$(k{-}1)$ data.

Given a text passage $x$, the task is to estimate its recursive generation depth $d \in \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$.
This is an ordinal classification problem: depths carry a natural ordering, and $d{=}1$ is structurally closer to $d{=}0$ than $d{=}3$ is, since each generation step compounds distributional drift.

\subsection{Multi-Generational Benchmark Construction}
\label{sec:method:benchmark}

We construct a benchmark spanning three axes: model family, decoding strategy, and generation depth.

\paragraph{Model families.}
We select three 1--1.5B-parameter models with diverse pretraining corpora and architectures: Pythia-1.4B (EleutherAI), OLMo-1B (AI2), and GPT-2~XL (OpenAI, 1.5B).
Restricting to a narrow parameter range isolates the effect of architecture and training data from scale.

\paragraph{Decoding strategies.}
Each model is paired with three decoding strategies: nucleus sampling ($p{=}0.95$) \cite{holtzman2020_nucleus}, temperature sampling ($\tau{=}0.9$), and top-$k$ sampling ($k{=}50$).
These span the dominant modes of open-ended generation and induce qualitatively different entropy profiles.
Our findings on feature importance and discriminability are limited to this grid; transfer to untested models, scales, or strategies is not established.

\paragraph{Depth and scale.}
The primary benchmark covers depths d0--d3 across all $3 \times 3 = 9$ model--strategy cells, with 5{,}000 samples per depth per cell (180K samples total).
For depth extension experiments (\S\ref{sec:atlas}), we extend one pipeline (Pythia-1.4B, nucleus) to d0--d5.

\paragraph{Generation pipeline.}
Starting from a Wikipedia subset ($\mathcal{D}_0$), each cycle: (1)~LoRA finetuning \cite{hu2022lora} on $\mathcal{D}_{k-1}$ ($r{=}16$, $\alpha{=}32$, lr $2{\times}10^{-4}$, effective batch 16, 3 epochs, dropout $0.05$; fresh adapter from the base model at each depth; full protocol in Appendix~\ref{app:lora_protocol}); (2)~sampling 5{,}000 passages (max 512 tokens, BOS-seeded) under the specified strategy; (3)~$\mathcal{D}_k$ feeds the next cycle.
Each depth is produced by a model trained on prior-depth data, not by sampling from the unmodified base model.
BOS-seeded generation may amplify depth signal; prompted-generation validation (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) confirms signal persistence ($-$13.8\,pp).
No per-passage lineage exists across depths; cross-validation splits do not introduce leakage.

\paragraph{Reference model.}
All statistical features (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) use Pythia-1.4B as fixed reference model.
This overlaps with one generator (Pythia), conferring a 4--11\,pp self-reference advantage on that pipeline (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}); Pythia results in Table~\ref{tab:main_matrix} are marked $^\dagger$ accordingly.
Alternative references yield 67--79\%, all exceeding $2.5{\times}$ chance (Limitations).

\subsection{Statistical Feature Design}
\label{sec:method:features}

We extract a feature vector $\mathbf{f}(x)$ comprising 19 candidate features from the reference-model perplexity distribution over each passage $x$; ablation (\S\ref{sec:atlas:features}) shows the top 5 achieve 97.6\% of full-set performance, and variance inflation analysis reveals VIF${>}$10 for 14--16 features; effective independent dimensionality is ${\approx}$4--5.
The features fall into four categories (Table~\ref{tab:features}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{The 19 statistical features comprising $\mathbf{f}(x)$, grouped by category. All perplexity and surprisal features are computed from the reference model (Pythia-1.4B).}
\label{tab:features}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llr@{}}
\toprule
Category & Features & $n$ \\
\midrule
Perplexity & mean, var, skewness, kurtosis, & 9 \\
statistics & p10, p25, p50, p75, p90 & \\
\addlinespace
Surprisal & mean, var, entropy & 3 \\
statistics & & \\
\addlinespace
Lexical & type--token ratio, hapax ratio, & 4 \\
diversity & bigram entropy, trigram entropy & \\
\addlinespace
Repetition & repeated 2/3/4-gram fractions & 3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\emph{Perplexity statistics} (9 features) capture the per-token perplexity distribution via moments and quantiles (p10--p90); tail quantiles are especially informative as recursive finetuning erodes the high-perplexity tail (\S\ref{sec:atlas}).
\emph{Surprisal statistics} (3 features): mean, variance, and entropy of $-\log p(\text{token})$.
\emph{Lexical diversity} (4 features): type--token ratio, hapax ratio, bigram/trigram entropy.
\emph{Repetition} (3 features): repeated $n$-gram fractions for $n \in \{2, 3, 4\}$.

\subsection{Effective Discriminable Depth}
\label{sec:method:edd}

Aggregate multi-class accuracy obscures whether discriminability degrades at specific depth boundaries.
We propose the \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} (EDD) as an operational measure of how far into a recursive chain adjacent depths remain distinguishable.

\paragraph{Pairwise discriminability.}
Let $\mathcal{D} = \{0, 1, \dots, K\}$ denote the depth set and $T \in (0.5, 1.0]$ a discrimination threshold.
For each adjacent pair $(d, d{+}1)$, we define the pairwise discriminability $\mathrm{AUC}(d, d{+}1)$ as the area under the ROC curve of a dedicated binary Random Forest classifier (500 trees, $\lfloor\sqrt{19}\rfloor$ candidate features per split) trained on class-balanced data from $\mathbf{f}(x)$ (\S\ref{sec:method:features}) to distinguish depth-$d$ from depth-$(d{+}1)$ samples, evaluated via 5-fold stratified cross-validation.
Each pair uses an independent classifier to avoid decision boundary dilution from multi-class settings.

\begin{definition}[Effective Discriminable Depth]
\label{def:edd}
The \emph{Effective Discriminable Depth} at threshold $T$ is:
\begin{multline}
\mathrm{EDD}_T = \max \bigl\{ d^*{+}1 \,:\, \\
\forall\, d' \leq d^*,\; \mathrm{AUC}(d', d'{+}1) \geq T \bigr\}
\label{eq:edd}
\end{multline}
where $d^* \in \{0, \dots, K{-}1\}$. If\, $\mathrm{AUC}(0,1) < T$, then $\mathrm{EDD}_T = 0$.
\end{definition}

$\mathrm{EDD}_T$ reports the longest \emph{contiguous} discriminable prefix: the deepest $d$ such that every pair from $(0,1)$ through $(d{-}1, d)$ exceeds $T$.
All 12 AUC profiles in our benchmark decay monotonically, empirically validating the monotonicity assumption; the sole non-monotonic case (an instruction-tuned model outside the primary atlas) yields $\mathrm{EDD}{=}0$, rendering the distinction between contiguous and non-contiguous discriminability moot for our configurations.
We report $T{=}0.70$ as primary threshold, following the AUC discrimination standard of \citet{hosmer2000} (8/9 cells achieve $\mathrm{EDD} \geq 3$); sensitivity at $T \in \{0.65, 0.70, 0.75, 0.80\}$ in \S\ref{sec:atlas}.

\paragraph{Classifiers.}
Our primary classifier is a Random Forest (200 trees, unconstrained depth, 5-fold CV).\footnote{EDD binary classifiers use 500 trees; the main 4-class RF uses 200 trees.}
The RF produces ordinally consistent predictions: 98.2\% of errors fall on adjacent depths ($|\Delta| \leq 1$), QWK\,=\,0.89; CORAL ordinal regression matches within 0.6\,pp ($p > 0.05$; \S\ref{sec:generalization}).
Comparisons against CORAL \cite{coral2020}, DivEye \cite{diveye2026}, and other baselines appear in \S\ref{sec:generalization}.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares ordinal depth estimation methods across all 9 atlas cells.
RF, XGBoost, and LightGBM on the same 19 features converge within 0.4\,pp per cell (a feature-determined ceiling); on Pythia nucleus, CORAL \cite{coral2020} (78.1\%) and DivEye \cite{diveye2026} (78.2\%) match RF within 0.6\,pp ($p>0.05$).

\paragraph{Neural end-to-end baselines.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (LoRA) underperforms RF by 16--32\,pp; depth-2 recall collapses to 8.4\%, indicating intermediate-depth distributional shifts are invisible to token-level encoders without explicit feature engineering.
RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal contrastive; 64.5\%) narrows the gap but remains 14\,pp below RF.

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Perturbation-based (DetectGPT, 28.8\%) and membership-inference (Min-K\% Prob, 33.2\%)\footnote{Min-K\%++ \cite{zhang2024_minkpp}, an improved variant, yields comparable ordinal performance.} methods produce scores with no ordinal resolution (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}); CORAL calibration does not close the gap (d1--d3: 40.4\% vs.\ RF 71.6\%).
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024}, calibrated with an 11-feature RF on per-token cross-perplexity scores, matches our pipeline within 0.3\,pp (78.1\% vs.\ 78.4\%, $p{>}0.05$); merging all 30 features yields 82.6\% (+4.2\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation_bino}).
Our pipeline requires a single reference model (Pythia-1.4B) with interpretable features, vs.\ the Binoculars observer--performer pair (1.8B total parameters) requiring model-pair selection.

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (DeepSeek V3.2, 36.2\%; Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct, 30.4\%) also fail (43\,pp below the best RF cell; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}); depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Ordinal depth estimation: method comparison. RF mean across 9 atlas cells (5-fold CV, 200 trees); DeBERTa-v3 range across 3 tested cells; DetectGPT: 9/9 cells; Binoculars: observer=Pythia-1.4B, performer=Pythia-410M, 9/9 cells. Chance = 25\% (4-class) / 33.3\% (d1--d3). $^\dagger$Adapted from binary detection; Binoculars 11-feat: $p{>}0.05$ vs.\ 19-feat RF (paired permutation test).}
\label{tab:baselines}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
Method & Calibration & 4-Cl (\%) & d1--d3 (\%) \\
\midrule
RF 19-feature (ours) & RF & 78.4 & \textbf{71.6} \\
Binoculars$^\dagger$ \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (11 feat) & 78.1 & --- \\
Merged (30 feat)$^\dagger$ & RF & \textbf{82.6} & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & threshold & 69.8 & --- \\
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024} & RF (1 feat) & 63.7 & --- \\
RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal) & contrastive & 64.5 & --- \\
DeBERTa-v3 (end-to-end) & cross-entropy & 53.1--60.6 & --- \\
\midrule
Zero-shot LLM (best) & --- & 36.2 & --- \\
Min-K\% Prob \cite{shi2024_minkprob} & CORAL & 33.2 & 40.4 \\
DetectGPT \cite{mitchell2023_detectgpt} & best-of-3 & 28.8 & 24.3 \\
Random & --- & 25.0 & 33.3 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Convergence confirms the signal resides in the features: three classifiers match within 0.8\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), perplexity features alone recover 66.0\%, and seed replication across three families shows $\Delta{\leq}3.2$\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:seed_replication}).
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 near-perfectly identified (recall ${>}$99\%); errors concentrate at d2/d3 in-distribution and spread to d1--d3 under transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.9\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d1--d3 bal.\ acc: Pythia 49.9\%, OLMo 59.2\%, GPT-2~XL 57.2\% (mean 55.4\%). Ordinal quality: MAE\,=\,0.39, QWK\,=\,0.81 (QWK$_{\text{d1--d3}}$\,=\,0.53); 94.8\% of errors at $|\Delta| \leq 1$.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy is 66.4\% (d1--d3: 55.4\%, $2.7{\times}$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline; d2 drops to 26.8\% worst case (Pythia holdout).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy} reports leave-one-strategy-out transfer.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Chance = 25\% / 33\% (d1--d3). d0 recall $\geq$89\% inflates headline; d1--d3 mean: 45.9\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Cross-strategy transfer yields 59.1\% (d1--d3: 45.9\%), 7\,pp below cross-model, reflecting structurally distinct degradation trajectories; a two-stage pipeline recovers 11.5\,pp but requires strategy identification (57.8\% 3-class; Appendix~\ref{app:extended_discussion}).
Topic-seeded generation (GPT-2~XL nucleus) yields 69.5\% ($-$13.8\,pp vs.\ BOS-seeded; Appendix~\ref{app:prompted_gen}).

%% ----------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Preliminary Cross-Scale Observations (14B)}
\label{sec:gen:crossscale}

Above-chance accuracy is observed at 14B (Qwen2.5-14B, FP16, self-reference) across all three strategies: nucleus 54.1\%, temperature 50.5\%, top-$k$ 47.9\% (mean 50.8\%, $2{\times}$ chance), but $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}0$ across all three, indicating adjacent-depth discrimination does not survive the scale transition; strategy-specific AUC patterns nonetheless confirm trajectory divergence (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).
Cross-domain arXiv achieves 74.1\% at the primary 1--1.5B scale (Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale_full}).

